{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nADR 0125 puts `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` in each verifying environment's own configuration, not the repository.\n\nNothing enforces that. `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` is an ordinary `[verify]` key (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py`, `VerifyConfig.trusted_opcert_keys`). The CI op-cert gate is the `verify-opcert` step of `.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml`, which runs `rebar verify-opcert` on the checked-out change. `cli()` in `src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py` calls `config.compose_config(root=args.root)` and `trusted_opcert_pairs(args.root)` (`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py:114`), and both resolve the project layer through `_discover_project_config` (`src/rebar/_config_sources.py:160`), which reads the working-tree `rebar.toml`. So a change under review that adds its own key to `rebar.toml`, drops `verify.require_environment`, or sets `[tracker] dir` to an empty directory (the gate also resolves its store through `config.tracker_dir(args.root)`, `verify_opcert.py:131`, and through `show_ticket`/`current_material_fingerprint`) loosens the very gate run that checks it.\n\n## Approach (operator decision, 2026-11-20)\n\nThe gate resolves its trust configuration from main's committed `rebar.toml`, never from the `rebar.toml` in the change under review.\n\nImplement this in-process in `rebar verify-opcert`, so it holds under any CI provider or none:\n\n- New module `src/rebar/_opcert_gate_config.py` with `gate_config(root) -> (Config, source, OperationSnapshot)`. It composes the same labelled layers as `load_config` (`src/rebar/config.py:468` `_compose`) — user config, project, env overrides, process `-c` overrides — but the project layer is the project config committed at the base ref, read with `git show <ref>:<path>`. It walks from the project root up to the git top level and takes the nearest `rebar.toml`, else a `pyproject.toml` with `[tool.rebar]`. `$REBAR_CONFIG` is ignored (it could name a working-tree file), so `_discover_project_config` is not reused. The working-tree project config is never read.\n- Base ref: `refs/remotes/origin/main` only, the ref attested gates already treat as reviewed main (`rebar.llm.gate_source.DEFAULT_REF`, `src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:71`). Failure handling, every arm:\n  - a remote exists but `origin/main` does not resolve: `ConfigError`, exit 2, naming the `git fetch` that fixes it;\n  - `ls-tree` fails, `git show` of a listed path fails (for example a blobless clone cannot fetch the blob), or the TOML does not parse: `ConfigError`, exit 2;\n  - the path is absent at the ref (decided by `git ls-tree --full-tree --name-only <ref> -- <path>`, which reads trees only): fall through to the next candidate, then to an empty project layer;\n  - `git remote` exits non-zero (not a repository, safe.directory refusal): `ConfigError`, exit 2;\n  - no remote at all (`git remote` succeeds and prints nothing): no reviewed branch and no change under review, so the working-tree configuration is kept.\n- `cli()` takes every gate value (`require_environment`, `opcert_enforce_since`, `trusted_opcert_keys`) from the `gate_config` result and calls neither `compose_config` nor `trusted_opcert_pairs(args.root)`; `trusted_opcert_pairs` is split so the gate parses the list it holds (`opcert_pairs(entries)`). It prints the trust source to stderr.\n- The same configuration is returned as an `OperationSnapshot` (`src/rebar/_operation_config.py:81`) and bound for the run with `bind_operation_snapshot`. That covers only the snapshot-aware readers, `config.tracker_dir` (`src/rebar/config.py:152`) and `_config_value`, so the store `show_ticket` and the material recomputation walk is main's `tracker.dir`. `load_config` does not consult the snapshot. Measured on the attack fixture, the one remaining working-tree read in the run is the advisory cross-session warning toggle (`_commands/cross_session.py:46`), whose errors `_lib_warn.emit_cross_session_warning` swallows; it cannot change the verdict. A test makes every working-tree project-config read raise during an in-process gate run and still requires main's `untrusted` verdict, so any new verdict-affecting read fails it.\n- `.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml`: the `verify-opcert` step fetches `refs/heads/main` into `refs/remotes/origin/main` (blobless, after the pack measurement) before running the gate.\n- Docs: the `verify-opcert` parser description (regenerated `src/rebar/_cli/help/*.txt` and `docs/cli-reference.md`), `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, `docs/manifest-signing.md`, `docs/exit-codes.md` (the new exit-2 cases), and `infra/runbooks/mcp-opcert-enforcement-flip.md` (an enforcement flip takes effect after it merges, not in its own gate run).\n\nOut of scope, stated so the boundary is explicit: the environment layer stays (ADR 0125 puts the trust list in the verifying environment), and keeping that layer, the gate's own code and the workflow file out of the change's reach belongs to the CI lane, not to this fix. The sibling `verify-identity` gate reads `identity.*` from the working tree the same way; that is a separate gate and is not changed here.\n\nNo config key, env var or flag is added: the base ref is fixed at `origin/main`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_opcert_gate_config.py` (new)\n- `src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py`\n- `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/verify.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/verify-opcert.txt`, `docs/cli-reference.md`\n- `.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml`\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, `docs/manifest-signing.md`, `docs/exit-codes.md`, `infra/runbooks/mcp-opcert-enforcement-flip.md`\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_gate_config.py` (new)\n\n## Testing\n\n`tests/unit/test_opcert_gate_config.py`, with a local bare repo as `origin`, covers: the end-to-end attack (exit 1 `untrusted` before merge, exit 0 after, and exit 0 on the old gate), an in-process run with working-tree config reads forced to raise, an unfetchable blob in a blobless clone, dirty and committed change configs ignored, `require_environment` not droppable, the environment trust list still applied, `pyproject.toml [tool.rebar]` on main, a redirected `tracker.dir`, a missing `origin/main` (exit 2 naming `git fetch origin`), and a remote-less checkout. The existing remote-less `tests/unit/test_verify_opcert*.py` fixtures pass unchanged. Full `make verify` before push.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The CI op-cert gate reads `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` from main's committed `rebar.toml`, not the change's: `.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml` fetches `origin/main` before `rebar verify-opcert`, and `gate_config` reads the project layer at that ref (`test_gate_config_ignores_the_working_tree_project_config`).\n- [ ] A change that adds a key to `rebar.toml` does not widen the trust list its own gate run uses: `test_change_adding_its_own_key_does_not_widen_its_gate_run` passes, and fails against the pre-fix `verify_opcert.py`.\n- [ ] The gate run reaches main's verdict with every working-tree project-config read raising: `test_gate_run_never_needs_the_working_tree_project_config` passes.\n- [ ] An unfetchable committed `rebar.toml` fails closed: `test_unfetchable_blob_on_main_fails_closed` passes.\n- [ ] A change setting `[tracker] dir` cannot redirect the store the gate walks: `test_change_cannot_redirect_the_store_it_walks` passes.\n- [ ] No config key, env var or flag is added: `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0 and `git diff origin/main -- src/rebar/_config_schema.py` is empty.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_gate_config.py tests/unit/test_verify_opcert.py tests/unit/test_verify_opcert_ac_coverage.py` passes."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795233778845907001,"uuid":"4ca26c8b-05d4-4fdf-9dde-b4e8d2a5ff26"}